def N(i, t): NOTES[i] = " ".join(t.split())
N(1, "This deck explains how a Bitcoin block is built, how blocks are linked and named, how a merkle tree lets one hash stand for many transactions, and how the test chains differ from the main chain. Every number on a result picture was computed with the chapter's own Python library or read from one recorded run of Bitcoin Core 31.1. Remember that a recorded run is one observation, not a general law. The seventeen code examples are listed in the same order in the Run slide code menu of the course page, where each one can be run and edited. The chapter follows the chapter on the blockchain in Mastering Bitcoin, third edition.")
N(2, "The route has three parts that follow the order of the chapter: the block and the chain, merkle trees, and test blockchains. A first box covers the questions and the concept map that open the deck. The numbers on the right say what to expect: 43 slides, four true stories and seventeen code examples, each with a picture of its result. Every slide is numbered at the bottom right, so a topic is easy to find again. The examples appear in the same order in the Run slide code menu of the course page, so every listing here can be run and changed there. Use this slide to find where a topic lives.")
N(3, "Seven questions frame the chapter, and each is answered on a named slide: the six header fields on slide 9, how a block is named and placed on slides 16 and 17, why one old change breaks later blocks on slide 18, how one root stands for many transactions on slide 22, how short a membership proof is on slide 24, how testnet, signet and regtest differ on slides 30 to 40, and why testnet4 replaced testnet3 on slides 32 and 33. The summary on slide 41 returns to them. A reader who can answer all seven in their own words has understood the chapter, so try each question before opening its slide.")
N(4, "The concept map shows how the chapter is divided. The block and the chain carry 17 key concepts and 8 examples, merkle trees carry 7 key concepts and 4 examples, and test blockchains carry 17 key concepts and 5 examples, 41 key concepts in all. Each part opens with a divider, continues with concept cards, and proves its ideas with code and a true story. The concept cards are short revision stops: each gives a meaning and points to the example that shows the idea. Use the map to see which part to revisit when an idea feels unclear, and compare it with the taxonomy of the course page.")
N(5, "A block is a header of 80 bytes followed by a list of transactions. This part takes the header apart, shows how a block is named and placed in the chain, and explains why changing one old block breaks every later block. The three columns preview the part: the anatomy of a block with examples 1 to 3, names and positions with examples 4 to 6, and links and forks with examples 7 and 8. Remember that each block names its parent by hash; that single habit turns separate blocks into a chain. Chapter 10 of the course describes the network that passes the blocks around.")
N(6, "These four cards define the vocabulary of a single block. A block is an 80-byte header, a count of transactions and then the transactions; the header holds six fixed fields that summarise what follows; a block has two names, the hash of its header and its height, which is its position counted from the genesis block; and the genesis block, mined on 3 January 2009, has no parent. Retell each card in your own words before moving on. Example 1 shows the structure, example 2 the header, examples 5 and 6 the two names and example 4 the genesis block. Compare with the chapter on the blockchain in Mastering Bitcoin.")
N(7, "The genesis block is the smallest block that matters: an 80-byte header, one count byte saying that there is one transaction, and a transaction of 204 bytes, 285 bytes in all. The code rebuilds the transaction from constants in Bitcoin Core's source, adds the three parts and compares the sum with the size that Bitcoin Core reports for its own genesis block on a private chain. Two equal numbers from two independent sources are the proof. This is example 1 in the Run slide code menu of the course page. It connects to slide 9, which takes the 80-byte header apart field by field.")
N(8, "These cards connect blocks into a chain. Each header holds the hash of its parent, so the blocks form one chain that every node keeps; a parent block is the block that a header names as the one before it; and the genesis block is the only block with no parent, its hash being fixed in the software. Because every link is a hash, a change to an old block changes its hash and breaks the link of the next block. Example 7 counts the links, example 8 shows two blocks that name the same parent, and example 4 shows the block without a parent. Compare with the fork on slide 19.")
N(9, "A block header always has the same six fields in the same order: version, previous block hash, merkle root, time, bits and nonce. Their widths are 4, 32, 32, 4, 4 and 4 bytes, and 4 plus 32 plus 32 plus three times 4 make 80. The code cuts the header of block 480 on the private chain at the positions 0, 4, 36, 68, 72, 76 and 80, so each width is the gap between two neighbouring cut points; it then reads the compact target, bits, from the same bytes and gets 207fffff. A fixed size makes headers cheap to store and check. This is example 2; the fields return in the merkle slides.")
N(10, "These cards prepare the fork example. Child blocks name an earlier block as their parent, and one parent may have several children; blocks are bundles of transactions with a header, added to the chain about every ten minutes; and the block header hash is the double SHA-256 of the 80 header bytes, which is the name of the block and is never printed inside it. Only one child continues the family line, and that line is the active chain. Example 8 shows two children of one parent and example 5 computes a block's name. Retell the difference between a block and its name in your own words.")
N(11, "The header carries three numbers about time and work. The time field counts seconds since 1 January 1970; the median time past is the middle value of the last eleven timestamps, so one wrong clock cannot move it; and bits is a compact form of the target, while difficulty says how many times smaller a target is than the easiest one. In the result the genesis time is 2009-01-03 18:15:05, the median time past is 1792052132, the easiest target has 224 bits, and the difficulty of bits 1903a30c is 1,180,923,195. A smaller target means more work. This is example 3; the mining chapter of Mastering Bitcoin explains difficulty further.")
N(12, "Block height is the number of blocks between a block and the genesis block, so the genesis block sits at height 0. Bitcoin Core is the reference software that checks every rule and stores the chain; in this chapter its recorded results are the reference against which the chapter's own code is compared. Satoshi Nakamoto is the pseudonymous creator of Bitcoin, who wrote the 2008 paper and mined the first block, whose story follows on slide 14. Nobody knows who is behind the name. Connect height with slide 17, where the height of a block is decoded from the first script of the block.")
N(13, "The first block quotes a newspaper headline: The Times of 3 January 2009, Chancellor on brink of second bailout for banks, a message of 69 characters stored in the source code. The block's name, 000000000019d668 and so on, starts with ten zero digits, which shows that very many nonces were tried before this header was found; that effort is the proof of work, and it is why the block is costly to forge. The time field is 1231006505. This is example 4, and the next slide tells the story of the headline.")
N(14, "On 3 January 2009, at 18:15:05 UTC, timestamp 1231006505, a person using the name Satoshi Nakamoto stamped the first block of a new kind of money, and inside it sat a headline from The Times of that day. A date written in a block proves that the block is not older than that day, and the headline hints at why an independent money was wanted. The chapter rebuilt the block from the constants in chainparams.cpp and hashed it to 000000000019d668; its 50 coins can never be spent. The picture is a statue in Budapest, not the creator, whose identity is unknown. The source line leads to the block on mempool.space, so the claim can be checked instead of believed.")
N(15, "These cards close the first part. Linking blocks means that each block commits to its parent's hash, so rewriting history means redoing the work of all later blocks. BIP 34 is the proposal that puts the block height at the start of every coinbase script, so anyone can read a block's position from its first transaction. The Bitcoin network is the peer-to-peer system of nodes that relay blocks and transactions; chapter 10 of the course describes it. Example 7 shows the cost of changing a block and example 6 reads BIP 34 heights. Retell how the three ideas depend on each other.")
N(16, "A block is named by hashing its 80-byte header twice with SHA-256. The code hashes once and gets a value that starts with 6dd6c5716cc8, which does not match the block's name; the second hash, taken over the first, starts with the zeros of the name and equals the hash in Bitcoin Core's source. The name is stored nowhere inside the block: every node recomputes it, so a name cannot be faked without doing the work. This is example 5. Compare with the ten zeros on slide 13 and with the parent link on slide 18.")
N(17, "Since BIP 34 the first bytes of the coinbase script carry the block's height, and the code reads the heights 1, 16, 17, 127, 128, 255, 256 and 500 back from eight recorded scripts. The second part of the example concerns maturity: a coinbase reward may be spent only after 100 confirmations, and Bitcoin Core refused to spend it after 99 confirmations and accepted it after 100. The waiting time protects against a block that is later replaced by another branch, as on slide 19. This is example 6; the wait is a consensus rule and not a wallet setting.")
N(18, "Each header holds the hash of its parent. In ten consecutive headers of the private chain nine links hold. When one bit in header 3 is flipped, the hash of header 3 changes, the link at header 4 breaks, and seven headers have to be mined again to make the chain valid. That cost is what protects old blocks: the older a block is, the more work an attacker must redo. This is example 7, the most important example of the part. It connects to slide 19, where two branches compete and the longer one becomes the active chain.")
N(19, "Two blocks mined on the same parent at height 511 form a fork. One more block on the first child made it the active chain, so the branch that grows longer wins. The last lines use the formula from the white paper to compute the chance that an attacker with ten percent of the mining power ever catches up: 1.0 from zero blocks behind, then 0.2045873, 0.0509779 and 0.0002428 for one, two and six blocks behind. The odds fall fast, which is why waiting for confirmations makes a payment safer. This is example 8; the fork connects back to the child blocks of slide 10.")
N(20, "A merkle tree squeezes thousands of transactions into one hash that sits in the header. This part builds a small tree, follows a short proof that one transaction is inside, and meets a weakness that comes from repeating the last hash. The three columns preview the part: the tree of hashes with example 9, short proofs with examples 10 and 12, and the weakness with example 11. Ralph Merkle's patent of 1982, told on slide 25, is the source of the idea. Remember that the header commits to the root, not to the list of transactions itself.")
N(21, "These cards define the tree. A merkle tree is a tree of hashes in which each parent hashes its two children and the top is a single root; in a binary tree every node has two children, so a tree over many leaves is only a few levels deep; a balanced tree pads an odd level by repeating its last hash so that every level pairs up; and a light client keeps only headers and asks for a short proof that a transaction is in a block. Example 9 builds the levels and example 12 shows the light client. The weakness of the padding is the subject of example 11 on slide 26.")
N(22, "Four transaction ids of a private block form the bottom level of the tree. Hashing them in pairs gives two hashes, and hashing those gives the root, which equals the value in the block header; the levels hold 4, 2 and 1 hashes. With three ids the last one is paired with itself, which gives levels of 3, 2 and 1 and still a single root. The padding detail causes the weakness shown in example 11. This is example 9. Compare the proof size on slide 24 and the light client on slide 27.")
N(23, "Three cards describe how a membership proof works. The authentication path is the list of sibling hashes from a leaf up to the root, and with them anyone can recompute the root; the merkle path is another name for the same list, one sibling hash for each level, so there are not two ideas; and lightweight clients are wallets that download headers only and verify transactions with merkle proofs. Example 10 builds a path and example 12 parses a proof sent to a light client. Retell in your own words why a verifier needs the siblings and not the whole list.")
N(24, "To prove that one of sixteen transactions is in a block, the prover hands over the sibling hash at each level: four hashes of 32 bytes, 128 bytes in all. Following them upward rebuilds the root in the header. The proof grows with the logarithm of the number of transactions, so 1,024 transactions need 320 bytes, 4,096 need 384 and 16,000 need 448, which is 14 hashes. Each doubling of the block adds a single hash. This is example 10, and the slow growth is what makes the light clients of slide 27 possible.")
N(25, "On 5 January 1982 the United States published patent 4,309,569, Method of providing digital signatures, by Ralph Merkle, with the Board of Trustees of Stanford University as assignee; his hash tree dates from 1979. The idea is small and strong: one short root vouches for a long list. Decades later the same idea sits in every Bitcoin block, where a header, which has room for one hash, covers every transaction. In block 277,315 the 40 transaction ids were hashed in pairs, six levels up, until a single root sat in the header. The source line names the patent number and the date, so the story can be checked.")
N(26, "Two different lists can have the same merkle root. Repeating the last two ids of six transactions gives a list with the same root, so a block that carries such a copy would look valid by its header. Core received a mutated block of four transactions in place of the original three, rejected it with the verdict bad-txns-duplicate, and the original stayed the tip. The defence works on the content of the block and not on its header. This is example 11, the first weakness of the tree. Compare the padding rule on slide 22 and the Optech topic on slide 42.")
N(27, "A light client asks a full node for a proof instead of the whole block. The merkleblock message holds the header, a count of transactions, five hashes and some flag bits, 248 bytes in all for a block of sixteen transactions, and it shows that one transaction is in the block. Bitcoin Core confirmed that the proof names exactly the transaction that was asked about. For blocks with thousands of transactions the saving is much larger, because a proof grows by only one hash for each doubling. This is example 12; it uses the path idea of slide 24.")
N(28, "Bitcoin has more than one chain. Testnet, signet and regtest follow the same rules as the main chain but use different constants, so developers can experiment without real money. This part compares the five chains and reads their constants, tells why testnet3 was replaced, and opens signet's challenge script. The three columns preview the part: the five chains in example 13, testnet and its flooding problem in example 14, and signet and regtest in examples 16 and 17. Remember that the rules are shared and only the constants and the value of the coins differ.")
N(29, "These cards introduce the test chains. Bitcoin's test blockchains are separate chains with Bitcoin's rules and worthless coins; testnet is the public one, and its coins have no value; using testnet means pointing a wallet or node at it and taking free coins from a faucet; and its minimum-difficulty rule let miners flood it with blocks, which is why testnet4 replaced it. Example 13 reads the constants, example 14 the rule, and the story on slide 33 tells the flood. Retell what is the same and what differs between a test chain and mainnet.")
N(30, "The constants of every chain live in one source file, chainparams.cpp. Each chain listens on its own default port: 8333 for mainnet, 18333 for the old testnet, 48333 for testnet4, 38333 for signet and 18444 for regtest, so nodes of different chains do not mix. The size of the data that a new node is assumed to download ranges from 856 gigabytes for main through 245 for test, 31 for testnet4 and 24 for signet down to 0 for regtest. This is example 13. The saved source files are listed in the references of the course page.")
N(31, "These cards describe the controlled test chains. Signet needs a signature from a trusted signer on every block, so the chain stays calm; one default signet is maintained and anyone can start another with their own challenge script; regtest is a private chain on your own computer where blocks are mined on demand; and development follows the order build on regtest, try on signet or testnet, release on mainnet. Example 16 opens the challenge script and example 17 shows the halving of regtest. Retell which test chain suits trying a wallet feature on a laptop, and why.")
N(32, "Testnet allows a block at the minimum difficulty after twenty idle minutes. The result shows that testnet, testnet4 and regtest allow this and that mainnet and signet do not. The rule keeps a quiet test chain moving, but it also let miners flood testnet3 with blocks. Bitcoin Core's help text now says that support for testnet3 is deprecated, and testnet4 starts again from a new genesis block whose hash starts with 00000000da. This is example 14; the story on slide 33 explains why the rule became a problem.")
N(33, "On 4 May 2024 Jameson Lopp published Griefing Bitcoin's Testnet and explained how the testnet3 rule, easy blocks after twenty idle minutes, let miners create hundreds of blocks in a single day. A rule meant to keep a small chain moving had become an invitation to vandals, because test coins cost nothing. Fabian Jahr's BIP 94 and testnet4 write rules against abuse into the chain itself; BIP 94 notes that three years of blocks were mined in a few weeks. On 15 October 2026 testnet3 stood at height 5,189,394 and testnet4 at 156,618. The source line leads to BIP 94. Compare signet on slide 38, another answer.")
N(34, "This recap defines three terms. Test blockchains are chains that follow Bitcoin's rules with different constants; testnet is the public test chain, and the current one is testnet4; signet is a test chain in which each block must carry a valid signature. The rules are the same as on mainnet, while the constants and the value of the coins differ. Examples 13 to 15 compare the constants, and example 16 opens the challenge that decides who may sign. Retell, without looking, what is the same and what differs between a test chain and mainnet.")
N(35, "Every chain writes its addresses with a prefix of its own. Mainnet uses bc for SegWit addresses and version byte 0 for legacy ones; testnet, testnet4 and signet use tb and 111; regtest uses bcrt and 111. Bitcoin Core made a real regtest address that starts with bcrt1q, so the prefix in the table is the one that the software produces. A wallet can reject an address of the wrong chain at once, which protects against sending real money to a test address. This is example 15; the earlier chapter on keys and addresses explains the formats.")
N(36, "These cards connect the signet vocabulary. The default signet is the public signet that most developers share; custom signets are created by choosing your own challenge script; and the challenge script is the script that a signet block's signature must satisfy. Changing the script gives another network, because the four magic bytes come from its hash. Example 16 opens the default challenge of 71 bytes. Retell how the challenge script, the chain and its magic bytes depend on each other.")
N(37, "The default signet's challenge is a short script of 71 bytes that asks for a signature from one of two keys, a one-of-two multisig. The four message-start bytes, 0a03cf40, are the start of the hash of that script, so a different challenge gives a different network: a challenge of 51, which is OP_TRUE and lets anyone sign, gives 54d26fbd. This is example 16. BIP 325 defines the rules, and the chapters on scripts and signatures explain multisig and the checks behind a signature.")
N(38, "On 20 March 2019 Karl-Johan Alm and Anthony Towns wrote BIP 325 for a test network in which mining is replaced by a signature: a block is valid only if it solves a challenge script, so the creators decide who may make blocks. Nobody can flood such a network, so it stays calm; the price is that open participation is given up for stability. The default challenge is a one-of-two multisig whose hash gives the four magic bytes 0a03cf40. Core 0.21.0 added signet, and on 15 October 2026 its tip was at height 326,440, 2.27 times the book's listing. The source line leads to BIP 325.")
N(39, "The last concept cards anchor three terms. Regtest is the regression-test mode: a private chain with trivial difficulty; BIP 325 is the proposal that defines signet; and mainnet is the real Bitcoin chain, where coins have value. Place each activity on the right chain: trying a script belongs on regtest, trying a shared setup on signet, and paying real money on mainnet. Example 17 shows the halving of regtest and example 15 shows that mainnet addresses start with bc. Retell how the three terms relate.")
N(40, "On regtest the reward halves every 150 blocks instead of every 210,000, so the whole schedule can be tested in seconds. The code feeds 150 to the library's rule and obtains 50, 50, 25, 25, 12.5 and 6.25 coins at the heights 1, 149, 150, 299, 300 and 450. The same rule gives the same numbers as the record of the 500 blocks that Bitcoin Core mined. A shorter schedule is a changed constant, not a different rule. This is example 17; the real schedule is explained in the mining chapter of Mastering Bitcoin.")
N(41, "These five lines are the chapter in miniature, and each can be proven from the code of its part. Line one is proven by the genesis and header slides, line two by the broken chain and the fork, line three by the merkle path, line four by the weakness slide and line five by the test chain slides. If a line feels unproven, return to that slide and run its example in the Run slide code menu of the course page; every figure was computed there. Compare your own answers to the seven questions of slide 3 with these lines.")
N(42, "Everything here is free to read or watch, and every link was opened on the date in the subtitle. The Optech topics explain merkle tree vulnerabilities and signet, mempool.space lets you look up any block on the real chain, BIP 94 and BIP 325 are the specifications behind testnet4 and signet, and the white paper is Nakamoto's nine pages of October 2008. The visual demo by Anders Brownworth shows hashing, blocks and chains step by step. The community entries, the weekly Optech newsletter, Delving Bitcoin and the bitcoin-dev mailing list, are where proposals are debated and questions get answered after the course.")
N(43, "Four claims close the chapter, and each is carried by a computation that ran on a slide of this deck: how a block is built and named on slides 7 to 16, why the chain resists change on slides 18 and 19, how a merkle proof works and where it is weak on slides 22 to 27, and which test chain to use, and when, on slides 30 to 40. The course page for chapter 11 has every word behind them, the stories with their sources, and a question bank to test yourself. Say each claim aloud in your own words, then find its slide to check it.")

def set_text(sh, text):
    ps = sh.text_frame.paragraphs; r = ps[0].runs
    r[0].text = text
    for x in r[1:]: x._r.getparent().remove(x._r)
    for p in ps[1:]: p._p.getparent().remove(p._p)

def walk(shs):
    for x in shs:
        if x.shape_type == T.GROUP: yield from walk(x.shapes)
        else: yield x

if __name__ == "__main__":
    p = Presentation(SRC)
    # 1. code frames: named 'code N' by position, long frames split (same logic as deck_patch_generic_v1_0_0.py, whose split_box is reused)
    src = open(os.path.join(HERE, "deck_patch_generic_v1_0_0.py"), encoding="utf-8").read()
    fn = src[src.index("def split_box"):src.index("E = 914400")]
    g = {"copy": copy, "W": W}
    exec(compile(fn, "split_box", "exec"), g)
    split_box = g["split_box"]; g["W"] = W
    for i, s in enumerate(p.slides, 1):
        codes = sorted([x for x in s.shapes if x.has_text_frame and x.name != "chip" and any(r.font.name == "Courier New" for pa in x.text_frame.paragraphs for r in pa.runs)], key=lambda x: (x.top, x.left))
        for k, sh in enumerate(codes, 1): sh.name = "code %d" % k
    for i, s in enumerate(p.slides, 1):
        for sh in [x for x in s.shapes if x.has_text_frame and x.name.startswith("code ") and "(part" not in x.name]:
            if W(sh.text_frame.text) > 30: split_box(s, sh)
    # 2. analogy lines, subtitles, footers
    for i, s in enumerate(p.slides, 1):
        think = [x for x in walk(s.shapes) if x.has_text_frame and x.text_frame.text.startswith("Think of")]
        if think or i in CARDS:
            assert len(think) == len(CARDS[i]), (i, len(think), len(CARDS.get(i, [])))
            for sh, t in zip(think, CARDS[i]): set_text(sh, t)
        for sh in walk(s.shapes):
            if sh.has_text_frame and sh.text_frame.text.strip() in SUBS: set_text(sh, SUBS[sh.text_frame.text.strip()])
    # 3. stand-in pictures replaced by the real logo (story slides 33 and 38)
    for i in (33, 38):
        s = p.slides[i - 1]
        for sh in list(s.shapes):
            if sh.shape_type == T.PICTURE:
                l, t, w, h = sh.left, sh.top, sh.width, sh.height
                sh._element.getparent().remove(sh._element)
                from PIL import Image
                iw, ih = Image.open(LOGO).size; sc = min(w / iw, h / ih)
                pw, ph = int(iw * sc), int(ih * sc)
                pic = s.shapes.add_picture(LOGO, l + (w - pw) // 2, t + (h - ph) // 2, pw, ph); pic.name = "Image 0"
                pic._element.nvPicPr.cNvPr.set("descr", "The Bitcoin logo")
            elif sh.has_text_frame and sh.text_frame.text.startswith("Children in a sandpit"): set_text(sh, "Bitcoin logo - Grayliptrot, public domain, Wikimedia Commons")
            elif sh.has_text_frame and sh.text_frame.text.startswith("Signet ring of Ramesses"): set_text(sh, "Bitcoin logo - Grayliptrot, public domain, Wikimedia Commons")
            elif sh.has_text_frame and sh.text_frame.text.startswith("Stand-in picture"): sh._element.getparent().remove(sh._element)
    # 4. notes
    for i, s in enumerate(p.slides, 1):
        nw = W(NOTES[i]); assert 60 <= nw <= 140, (i, nw)
        s.notes_slide.notes_text_frame.text = NOTES[i]
    p.core_properties.version = "2.5.0"
    p.save(OUT); print("wrote", OUT)
